  "headline": "Rate-driven reflation: copper and bitcoin run, yields near 5%, dollar paid for it",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as a hot, US-led reflation. Copper sits at a 52-week high, up 7% this week and 22% year-to-date, and the copper/gold ratio has climbed 11.5% over the month, a clean growth-over-fear signal. Bitcoin is up 12% on the month while gold fell 6%, so one bitcoin buys 26% more gold than a month ago: that is liquidity and risk appetite, not hedging.",
    "The cost of that growth shows up in rates. The 10-year is up 23 basis points on the month to just under 5%, at the top of its range, while the 30-year added only 3; the curve flattened 20 basis points. A bear flattening led by the 10-year says the market is pricing a higher near-term policy path and inflation, not a long-run fiscal scare. Gold falling alongside rising yields and a firmer dollar confirms the move is about real rates, not stress. Brent's 62% year-to-date gain is the inflation impulse; this week's 8.6% drop eases one input without changing it.",
    "The tension is the dollar. Reflation usually softens it, yet the index is up 0.9% this week and 1.8% on the month, and USD/CAD rose 1.8% despite oil's monthly gains. That says the reflation is US-centric and the dollar is being paid for yield. Copper making highs in a strengthening dollar outweighs that caveat: real demand is doing the work."
